1
00:00:02,909 --> 00:00:19,161
[SPEAKER_02]: So, previously, I was taught about brahmaviharas, and I wanted to come here and find some clarity in regard to them, and I found a sutta that speaks on them.

2
00:00:19,502 --> 00:00:24,485
[SPEAKER_01]: It's called Removing Resentment, and that's how it's translated in Guttur-Nikaya 161-161.

3
00:00:27,777 --> 00:00:38,364
[SPEAKER_01]: In bhikkhus, there are these five ways of removing a resentment, by which a bhikkhu should entirely remove resentment when it has arisen towards... toward anyone.

4
00:00:38,404 --> 00:00:38,964
[SPEAKER_01]: What five?

5
00:00:39,925 --> 00:00:42,746
[SPEAKER_01]: One should develop love and kindness for the person one resents.

6
00:00:43,146 --> 00:00:46,348
[SPEAKER_01]: In this way, one should remove the resentment toward that person.

7
00:00:47,109 --> 00:00:51,952
[SPEAKER_01]: One should develop compassion and... and so... one should develop equanimity.

8
00:00:53,395 --> 00:00:57,746
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, one should regard the person one resents and pay no attention to him.

9
00:00:58,007 --> 00:01:00,432
[SPEAKER_01]: In this way, one should remove that resentment.

10
00:01:01,355 --> 00:01:02,036
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, one...

11
00:01:02,825 --> 00:01:07,228
[SPEAKER_01]: should apply the idea of ownership of kāma to the person one resents.

12
00:01:07,708 --> 00:01:12,311
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, this venerable one is the owner of his kāma, the heir of his kāma.

13
00:01:12,631 --> 00:01:15,432
[SPEAKER_01]: He has his kāma as his origin, relative, resort.

14
00:01:16,793 --> 00:01:18,814
[SPEAKER_01]: He is the heir of his kāma, good or bad.

15
00:01:19,195 --> 00:01:22,637
[SPEAKER_01]: In this way, one should remove the resentment toward that person.

16
00:01:23,717 --> 00:01:29,200
[SPEAKER_01]: These are the five ways in which removing resentment, by which bhikkhu should entirely remove it.

17
00:01:30,501 --> 00:01:31,242
[SPEAKER_01]: I was wondering if

18
00:01:33,433 --> 00:01:44,799
[SPEAKER_01]: It could be, I guess, more in regard to metta, compassion, equanimity... Well, yeah, that's the thing.

19
00:01:45,219 --> 00:01:52,583
[SPEAKER_00]: Metta is translated as loving-kindness, but it's quite a, actually, silly translation.

20
00:01:52,623 --> 00:01:53,723
[SPEAKER_00]: You're lovingly kind.

21
00:01:55,424 --> 00:01:56,325
[SPEAKER_00]: It means friendliness.

22
00:01:58,966 --> 00:02:00,867
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like, imagine if you're friends.

23
00:02:03,277 --> 00:02:06,038
[SPEAKER_00]: Imagine if a stranger is saying something hurtful to you.

24
00:02:06,818 --> 00:02:08,518
[SPEAKER_00]: Who do you think you are?

25
00:02:08,538 --> 00:02:09,119
[SPEAKER_00]: You get upset.

26
00:02:10,179 --> 00:02:13,860
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, imagine if your friend starts saying something, like your really, really good friend.

27
00:02:15,040 --> 00:02:21,341
[SPEAKER_00]: It's going to be much harder to get upset, because you experience that person through the framework of friendliness.

28
00:02:22,021 --> 00:02:22,741
[SPEAKER_00]: They are your friend.

29
00:02:24,982 --> 00:02:25,862
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the difference.

30
00:02:26,342 --> 00:02:29,283
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not what they said, and how they said it, and how you reacted.

31
00:02:30,167 --> 00:02:35,349
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the context of that saying that's basically something your mind is responsible for.

32
00:02:36,810 --> 00:02:41,812
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to practice metta, as in you want to develop the framework of friendliness towards all beings.

33
00:02:43,672 --> 00:02:45,993
[SPEAKER_00]: All beings are your friends, even the ones that want to harm you.

34
00:02:46,533 --> 00:02:48,234
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot possibly experience your will.

35
00:02:49,034 --> 00:02:52,436
[SPEAKER_00]: But loving-kindness, it's more of like a smarmy thing to do.

36
00:02:53,096 --> 00:02:54,296
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, but I love them.

37
00:02:54,336 --> 00:02:55,657
[SPEAKER_00]: They hurt, but I love you back.

38
00:02:56,347 --> 00:02:56,507
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

39
00:02:56,587 --> 00:02:57,867
[SPEAKER_00]: That's you overshooting the mark.

40
00:02:58,248 --> 00:02:59,088
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't need to love them.

41
00:02:59,188 --> 00:03:00,308
[SPEAKER_00]: You just need to not hate them.

42
00:03:01,789 --> 00:03:02,929
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the dhamma part of verse.

43
00:03:03,469 --> 00:03:05,570
[SPEAKER_00]: It says hatred is not abandoned by hatred.

44
00:03:06,630 --> 00:03:09,991
[SPEAKER_00]: Only by non-hatred you can abandon hatred.

45
00:03:10,131 --> 00:03:14,553
[SPEAKER_00]: And people often jump to translate that by saying by love you abandon hatred.

46
00:03:14,573 --> 00:03:14,693
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

47
00:03:14,853 --> 00:03:16,293
[SPEAKER_00]: You abandon love by non-love.

48
00:03:17,474 --> 00:03:20,014
[SPEAKER_00]: You abandon hatred by non-hatred.

49
00:03:20,054 --> 00:03:22,115
[SPEAKER_00]: Love is an equal impediment as hatred is.

50
00:03:25,507 --> 00:03:26,968
[SPEAKER_00]: So, think of metta as friendliness.

51
00:03:27,188 --> 00:03:31,332
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why, if you're of friendly disposition, you can't hate anyone.

52
00:03:31,852 --> 00:03:37,037
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the natural compassion already.

53
00:03:37,177 --> 00:03:44,483
[SPEAKER_00]: If your perspective is not restricted with your anger and your conceit, you already... that perspective in itself is what carries compassion.

54
00:03:45,284 --> 00:03:48,987
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you have a better sight, better... again, better perspective on what's going on.

55
00:03:50,961 --> 00:03:55,384
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why also that then culminates really in equanimity for Brahma-Vihāras.

56
00:03:56,345 --> 00:04:01,328
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're friendly, if you're compassionate, you'll be indifferent to whatever that person chooses to do.

57
00:04:02,569 --> 00:04:09,374
[SPEAKER_00]: He will not prompt your will in you, he will not prompt lust in you, so you will be equanimous, imperturbable.

58
00:04:10,797 --> 00:04:12,357
[SPEAKER_02]: I've seen a...

59
00:04:12,838 --> 00:04:16,739
[SPEAKER_02]: I guess compassion, the opposite of compassion, is not cruelty.

60
00:04:17,599 --> 00:04:22,581
[SPEAKER_02]: Or, I suppose, it's not the desire to... Yeah, yeah, compassion is a translation.

61
00:04:22,601 --> 00:04:24,121
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you can say it's a non-cruelty.

62
00:04:25,381 --> 00:04:40,346
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that would be the difference between this friendliness and compassion, is... the friendliness is in the lens of seeing whoever it is as a friend, and compassion in regard to any sort of

63
00:04:41,043 --> 00:04:43,324
[SPEAKER_00]: It's even more, yeah, more encompassing.

64
00:04:43,664 --> 00:04:46,184
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it's even more encompassing than just friendliness.

65
00:04:46,984 --> 00:04:49,425
[SPEAKER_00]: Friendliness is the basis, and it's a sufficient basis.

66
00:04:49,485 --> 00:04:57,146
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, you can just develop metta and experience complete dispassion, eventually, as a result of that framework.

67
00:04:58,047 --> 00:04:59,187
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the thing.

68
00:04:59,227 --> 00:05:08,929
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you were to take it further, the same principle, and you develop compassion, it would just be extended even further, in a sense of

69
00:05:10,169 --> 00:05:22,874
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not even like a... it's not just about the person, but even whatever they were to do, you would still maintain compassion, as in you would still act and make the effort, if it comes to it, for their well-being.

70
00:05:24,075 --> 00:05:26,096
[SPEAKER_00]: As opposed to acting out of their well-being.

71
00:05:26,336 --> 00:05:26,856
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

72
00:05:26,876 --> 00:05:33,379
[SPEAKER_00]: You wouldn't be acting in response to what they're doing, because you wouldn't lose perspective, because you are already established upon the framework of friendliness.

73
00:05:33,999 --> 00:05:34,399
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I mean.

74
00:05:34,419 --> 00:05:36,200
[SPEAKER_00]: The compassion would naturally come out of it.

75
00:05:38,209 --> 00:05:41,370
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're not compassionate when you lose the greater picture.

76
00:05:43,271 --> 00:05:44,951
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, like that, you have an experience...

77
00:05:45,932 --> 00:05:52,214
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know, somebody says something and you get angry and then somebody tells you, like, oh, I don't... you know, his parents just died or something.

78
00:05:52,254 --> 00:05:55,675
[SPEAKER_00]: And then suddenly you feel compassionate, although nothing has really changed.

79
00:05:55,755 --> 00:05:58,216
[SPEAKER_00]: He's still insulting you and saying bad things to you, I don't know.

80
00:05:58,876 --> 00:06:02,837
[SPEAKER_00]: But now you're not angry with him, because you kind of understood that, like, context that he's in pain.

81
00:06:04,538 --> 00:06:05,578
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you can't have the anger.

82
00:06:06,258 --> 00:06:08,219
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have anger when you don't have perspective.

83
00:06:08,560 --> 00:06:13,303
[SPEAKER_00]: But now, imagine you develop the ultimate perspective that doesn't even depend on the context in regard to that person.

84
00:06:13,563 --> 00:06:22,650
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if his parents didn't die, even if he's not in the pain, if he's just being an utter complete idiot, rude, whatever, offensive, he's still a being.

85
00:06:23,070 --> 00:06:25,872
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's like that beings are owners of their karma.

86
00:06:29,157 --> 00:06:33,319
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like something you need to psychologize and, like, oh, he's the owner, he'll pay the price.

87
00:06:33,359 --> 00:06:33,439
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

88
00:06:33,459 --> 00:06:45,864
[SPEAKER_00]: It literally means, recognizing that knowledge, that beings are bound with a karma, means, oh, he's subjected to his own actions, to his own lack of wisdom, to his own ignorance, to his own passions, completely unaware of it.

89
00:06:46,844 --> 00:06:50,606
[SPEAKER_00]: And you cannot be upset on that account, because that's what ignorant beings are.

90
00:06:53,647 --> 00:06:56,188
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in that sense, you wouldn't have any animosity towards them.

91
00:07:05,385 --> 00:07:14,774
[SPEAKER_02]: I have another sutta, the next one following it, also called Removing Resentment, and it says, it goes through five ways.

92
00:07:15,074 --> 00:07:20,599
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, before you go for the next sutta, that's why also that friendliness, you practice towards all beings, animals too.

93
00:07:21,480 --> 00:07:22,361
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the same framework.

94
00:07:23,001 --> 00:07:23,242
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

95
00:07:23,862 --> 00:07:28,427
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously, most often these things are prompted in people by interacting with other people, but it's the same.

96
00:07:30,168 --> 00:07:30,569
[SPEAKER_00]: Animals,

97
00:07:31,595 --> 00:07:37,596
[SPEAKER_00]: Pleasant animals, unpleasant animals, the ones you fear, the ones you hate, the ones you like, the ones you dislike.

98
00:07:38,997 --> 00:07:39,657
[SPEAKER_00]: Same with people.

99
00:07:40,737 --> 00:07:47,559
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the mind of friendliness means mind of non-harm, because you wouldn't harm a friend.

100
00:07:48,079 --> 00:07:49,139
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

101
00:07:49,840 --> 00:07:59,682
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if the friend's trying to bite you, you wouldn't lose the context of that he is your friend.

102
00:08:03,216 --> 00:08:04,440
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm trying to think in regard to...

103
00:08:04,621 --> 00:08:06,326
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, like, how I've seen...

104
00:08:09,046 --> 00:08:10,948
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like the action, or the reaction.

105
00:08:11,108 --> 00:08:12,189
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the reaction, yeah.

106
00:08:12,209 --> 00:08:18,694
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, oh, you practice compassion by spreading love, by spreading this, by beaming it, by radiating it.

107
00:08:18,954 --> 00:08:22,557
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, I've also seen it as, like, wishing for that person's... Yeah, yeah, yeah.

108
00:08:22,617 --> 00:08:23,378
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, wishing well.

109
00:08:23,498 --> 00:08:25,200
[SPEAKER_00]: Well-wishing is your metta practice.

110
00:08:25,860 --> 00:08:26,361
[SPEAKER_00]: No, actually.

111
00:08:26,381 --> 00:08:26,961
[SPEAKER_00]: Well-wishing...

112
00:08:27,261 --> 00:08:27,502
[SPEAKER_00]: Sorry.

113
00:08:27,762 --> 00:08:35,128
[SPEAKER_00]: Metta practice is understanding what nature of friendliness is, what that framework is, and then cultivating it.

114
00:08:35,988 --> 00:08:38,889
[SPEAKER_00]: Regardless of whether somebody is upsetting you or not.

115
00:08:39,569 --> 00:08:47,992
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the Buddha says, the Buddha said, oh, he would go, sit down, establish his mindfulness to the mouth, and then enter jhāna.

116
00:08:49,192 --> 00:08:53,014
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, he would come back from penance, sit down, establish his mindfulness to the mouth, and the second, third, fourth.

117
00:08:53,534 --> 00:08:58,596
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, he would come back from the penance, establish his mindfulness to the mouth, and practice development for Brahma-Vihāras.

118
00:09:01,316 --> 00:09:02,817
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's something you do when you're on your own.

119
00:09:03,750 --> 00:09:17,013
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you... if your mind is developed in that framework, that everybody is your friend, regardless of their actions and words, then when you do encounter people, they will be within that framework automatically.

120
00:09:17,693 --> 00:09:18,533
[SPEAKER_00]: They will be friends.

121
00:09:18,573 --> 00:09:19,633
[SPEAKER_00]: And you don't need to do anything.

122
00:09:19,653 --> 00:09:21,594
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't need to make any effort towards them.

123
00:09:21,614 --> 00:09:24,334
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's not what friendship is.

124
00:09:24,414 --> 00:09:26,235
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the attitude of the mind where the friendship is.

125
00:09:26,255 --> 00:09:27,295
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I mean by framework.

126
00:09:28,410 --> 00:09:29,370
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

127
00:09:29,710 --> 00:09:30,870
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, but I need to look in it.

128
00:09:31,611 --> 00:09:34,851
[SPEAKER_00]: May my mother... Of course you wish your mother to be well.

129
00:09:34,891 --> 00:09:36,131
[SPEAKER_00]: May my friends be well.

130
00:09:36,291 --> 00:09:39,432
[SPEAKER_00]: Why do you keep repeating this mechanically?

131
00:09:39,532 --> 00:09:49,634
[SPEAKER_00]: Understand what... Take your friend, take your mother, take people that you regard with friendly eye, and understand the nature of friendliness, regardless of the content of the people.

132
00:09:50,334 --> 00:09:52,474
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why brahmavihāras are lofty abidings.

133
00:09:53,375 --> 00:09:55,435
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you actually transcend the content of

134
00:09:56,274 --> 00:09:58,556
[SPEAKER_00]: Engagement, passion, your will, all of that.

135
00:09:59,556 --> 00:10:01,698
[SPEAKER_00]: You do it rightly, you will abandon sensuality as well.

136
00:10:03,339 --> 00:10:10,484
[SPEAKER_02]: Brahmavihāras, were they exclusive to only, I guess, the disciples or the noble ones, the Buddha?

137
00:10:10,504 --> 00:10:13,847
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you need to have a right view for any type of mind establishment.

138
00:10:15,107 --> 00:10:17,389
[SPEAKER_00]: But that doesn't mean you shouldn't be trying to do it rightly.

139
00:10:20,470 --> 00:10:26,054
[SPEAKER_02]: So, because, I mean, I mean, thinking about in regard to other ascetics with jhāna... Well, I don't know.

140
00:10:26,715 --> 00:10:27,095
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know.

141
00:10:27,115 --> 00:10:39,604
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't think it was mentioned that much in the suttas in that sense, but I would assume there are probably people who cultivated, you know, the compassion towards the whole world, plants, inanimate objects.

142
00:10:40,285 --> 00:10:44,828
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, from the ultimate point of view of arahantship, it's kind of... well, it's not adequate.

143
00:10:45,209 --> 00:10:45,389
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

144
00:10:45,569 --> 00:10:48,911
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's probably still quite a decent mind development.

145
00:10:50,248 --> 00:10:52,329
[SPEAKER_00]: To not be able to hate anything or anyone.

146
00:10:54,670 --> 00:10:55,831
[SPEAKER_00]: That's already quite something.

147
00:10:56,951 --> 00:10:58,592
[SPEAKER_02]: By having... having right view...

148
00:10:58,612 --> 00:11:00,433
[SPEAKER_00]: I think even Jesus might have been teaching that.

149
00:11:01,853 --> 00:11:02,514
[SPEAKER_00]: In a nutshell.

150
00:11:04,295 --> 00:11:06,856
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I think, you know, people get some recognition of it.

151
00:11:07,516 --> 00:11:18,982
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, you had those other Indian sort of holy men and it was often... often revolving around that non-harmfulness, the Ahimsa principle, the Jains, yeah.

152
00:11:19,182 --> 00:11:19,822
[SPEAKER_00]: But, eh...

153
00:11:20,805 --> 00:11:27,588
[SPEAKER_00]: But, yeah, I think the way the Buddha was teaching is more like actually properly done, in a sense of properly established on the nature of that thing.

154
00:11:27,628 --> 00:11:28,448
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you understand it.

155
00:11:28,508 --> 00:11:35,611
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you don't need to be emulating or going through the motions of expressing it, so to speak.

156
00:11:37,432 --> 00:11:45,735
[SPEAKER_02]: Having... using the cultivation of brahmaharas in regard to right view for uprooting conceit and sensuality.

157
00:11:45,755 --> 00:11:45,855
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

158
00:11:45,975 --> 00:11:48,756
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, imagine now, like you go back to Kuti and you... like again,

159
00:11:50,278 --> 00:12:04,285
[SPEAKER_00]: Recall a friend, something in your mind, but not the specific character or person, but just, like, try to ponder and discern the signs and characteristics of that friendship aspect, the nature of the... just friendship in general.

160
00:12:05,826 --> 00:12:11,449
[SPEAKER_00]: How... what is your mind like when it's... when it's experiencing a phenomenon of friendliness?

161
00:12:12,870 --> 00:12:16,552
[SPEAKER_00]: The more familiar you become with that friendliness, the more established that phenomenon is.

162
00:12:18,173 --> 00:12:22,536
[SPEAKER_00]: Regardless of who you have to deal with, it will be seen through that phenomenon.

163
00:12:23,817 --> 00:12:26,419
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why you will be unable to hate them.

164
00:12:26,479 --> 00:12:27,460
[SPEAKER_00]: Not because you love them.

165
00:12:28,361 --> 00:12:29,242
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't need to love them.

166
00:12:30,362 --> 00:12:34,125
[SPEAKER_00]: As I said, you can just... All you need to do is not give in to hate.

167
00:12:34,726 --> 00:12:34,866
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

168
00:12:34,926 --> 00:12:37,728
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, and the term love is always...

169
00:12:38,008 --> 00:12:44,513
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, how I always see it is like if something's taken away, if that thing that I love is taken, then there's going to be sadness emerging from it.

170
00:12:44,593 --> 00:12:44,813
[SPEAKER_02]: It is.

171
00:12:45,014 --> 00:12:46,555
[SPEAKER_00]: And I think it is like almost like a

172
00:12:47,522 --> 00:13:01,156
[SPEAKER_00]: Semi-intentional effort to blur the line between love, which is an inevitable infatuation, attachment, requirement, need, and the spiritual love, which is clean of that and pure of that.

173
00:13:01,176 --> 00:13:01,956
[SPEAKER_00]: That's impossible.

174
00:13:03,798 --> 00:13:06,281
[SPEAKER_00]: If it's love, means you're overshooting the mark.

175
00:13:07,141 --> 00:13:09,083
[SPEAKER_00]: If it's hate, means you're overshooting the mark.

176
00:13:09,984 --> 00:13:11,185
[SPEAKER_02]: You're not seeing the middle of it.

177
00:13:13,692 --> 00:13:16,094
[SPEAKER_00]: And the people think, well, the Buddha had the love.

178
00:13:16,434 --> 00:13:16,974
[SPEAKER_00]: No, he didn't.

179
00:13:17,274 --> 00:13:18,275
[SPEAKER_00]: He didn't even want to teach.

180
00:13:19,196 --> 00:13:20,136
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, it's a hassle.

181
00:13:20,496 --> 00:13:21,977
[SPEAKER_00]: Why would I waste my time?

182
00:13:22,838 --> 00:13:24,819
[SPEAKER_00]: Eight out of ten people won't understand what I'm saying.

183
00:13:26,760 --> 00:13:36,186
[SPEAKER_00]: And when the Supreme Brahma then came and asked him three times, begged him to do it, because, well, at least two out of ten people will understand, and to that extent, it's a great thing.

184
00:13:36,246 --> 00:13:40,269
[SPEAKER_00]: And then the Buddha decided, okay, he'll teach, not because he loved them, but because he had compassion

185
00:13:41,014 --> 00:13:48,898
[SPEAKER_00]: In the same sense, a doctor who knows the cure to the illness of the humanity, humankind, would decide to share his medicine.

186
00:13:49,998 --> 00:13:57,981
[SPEAKER_00]: But he said himself, like, when the man asked him, oh, do you get elated when your disciples, like, you know, fulfill your teachings?

187
00:13:58,542 --> 00:13:59,802
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you get sad when they don't?

188
00:14:00,382 --> 00:14:01,583
[SPEAKER_00]: And he said, let me ask in return.

189
00:14:01,623 --> 00:14:05,405
[SPEAKER_00]: Somebody comes and asks you for a way to a certain village.

190
00:14:05,445 --> 00:14:07,285
[SPEAKER_00]: And you tell him, well, at this junction, take left.

191
00:14:09,066 --> 00:14:10,647
[SPEAKER_00]: And then that person takes right.

192
00:14:12,155 --> 00:14:13,316
[SPEAKER_02]: Are you upset on account of that?

193
00:14:13,356 --> 00:14:14,036
[SPEAKER_00]: He says, no.

194
00:14:14,296 --> 00:14:15,717
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, I've done my bit.

195
00:14:16,557 --> 00:14:18,098
[SPEAKER_00]: But he chose to go the opposite way.

196
00:14:18,118 --> 00:14:19,298
[SPEAKER_00]: I say, well, so it's with the target.

197
00:14:19,418 --> 00:14:20,079
[SPEAKER_00]: I've done my bit.

198
00:14:20,879 --> 00:14:23,060
[SPEAKER_00]: The person chooses not to follow it.

199
00:14:23,440 --> 00:14:25,741
[SPEAKER_00]: That's displeasing in a way, like I wasted my time.

200
00:14:26,601 --> 00:14:30,823
[SPEAKER_00]: But that displeasure does not overcome and obsess and sort of possess my mind.

201
00:14:31,744 --> 00:14:34,925
[SPEAKER_00]: Then somebody does what I suggest and what I instruct them to do.

202
00:14:35,505 --> 00:14:39,707
[SPEAKER_00]: Then I'm pleased, because they follow my instructions through and through and I didn't waste my time talking to them.

203
00:14:40,287 --> 00:14:41,368
[SPEAKER_00]: But that pleasure does not.

204
00:14:41,840 --> 00:14:44,382
[SPEAKER_00]: Come over my mind and obsess it and persist it and so on.

205
00:14:46,124 --> 00:14:48,906
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, it's a compassion of... yeah, I can help you.

206
00:14:49,367 --> 00:14:51,729
[SPEAKER_00]: And here is the cure, here is the medicine, here is the diet.

207
00:14:52,750 --> 00:14:56,873
[SPEAKER_00]: Here is a strict set of rules you have to, like, adhere to and follow what I say.

208
00:14:57,694 --> 00:14:58,254
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't do it?

209
00:14:58,675 --> 00:15:00,196
[SPEAKER_00]: How could I possibly care about that?

210
00:15:00,236 --> 00:15:00,897
[SPEAKER_00]: Not my problem.

211
00:15:01,777 --> 00:15:02,178
[SPEAKER_00]: You do it?

212
00:15:02,238 --> 00:15:02,458
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

213
00:15:02,538 --> 00:15:02,999
[SPEAKER_00]: Good for you.

214
00:15:03,199 --> 00:15:03,659
[SPEAKER_00]: There you go.

215
00:15:03,679 --> 00:15:04,540
[SPEAKER_00]: Now you see for yourself.

216
00:15:05,803 --> 00:15:06,263
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's it.

217
00:15:06,684 --> 00:15:16,471
[SPEAKER_00]: But people like, again, as I said, with these blurred lines between our love and love, because it allows you to indulge, then, this great compassion love, which is all revolves around your conceit as well.

218
00:15:18,613 --> 00:15:22,776
[SPEAKER_00]: And how the Buddha loved all of us, and we, no, we most certainly did not.

219
00:15:25,203 --> 00:15:31,186
[SPEAKER_02]: It reminds me of the one sutra where the Buddha says, like, to the horse trainer, like, I kill them.

220
00:15:31,547 --> 00:15:38,471
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, those that don't, you know, after I've tried to train them, and they still don't listen to me, well, then I kill them.

221
00:15:38,531 --> 00:15:38,811
[SPEAKER_02]: How?

222
00:15:38,911 --> 00:15:40,472
[SPEAKER_02]: By not talking to them.

223
00:15:40,532 --> 00:15:42,053
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's also one... By being finished with them, yeah.

224
00:15:42,153 --> 00:15:44,194
[SPEAKER_02]: And removing resentment also in regard to...

225
00:15:44,214 --> 00:15:44,874
[SPEAKER_02]: Ignoring, yeah.

226
00:15:45,034 --> 00:15:45,555
[SPEAKER_02]: Ignoring the person.

227
00:15:45,575 --> 00:15:47,275
[SPEAKER_00]: Paying no attention to it, yeah.

228
00:15:52,498 --> 00:15:52,979
[SPEAKER_02]: I've seen...

229
00:15:54,327 --> 00:16:09,120
[SPEAKER_02]: In the following sutta, it talks about... there's a person... people of different bodily behavior, impure or verbal behavior, that is pure, and regarding them as such.

230
00:16:11,577 --> 00:16:14,599
[SPEAKER_02]: Some of them have a placidity of mind.

231
00:16:15,340 --> 00:16:23,447
[SPEAKER_02]: I've seen a certain portion of this sutta quoted in regard to compassion or cultivating compassion.

232
00:16:25,288 --> 00:16:26,309
[SPEAKER_02]: I'll read that line now.

233
00:16:26,429 --> 00:16:36,578
[SPEAKER_02]: It says, "...how, friends, should resentment be removed toward a person whose bodily and verbal behavior are impure and who does not gain an opening of mind, placidity of mind, from time to time?"

234
00:16:37,518 --> 00:16:54,390
[SPEAKER_02]: as to suppose a sick afflicted gravely ill person was traveling along a highway from the last village behind him in the next ahead of him were both far away he would not obtain suitable food and medicine or a qualified attendant he would not get to meet the leader of the village district

235
00:16:55,270 --> 00:17:08,376
[SPEAKER_02]: Another man traveling on that highway might see him and arouse sheer compassion, sympathy and tender concern for him, thinking, oh, may this man obtain suitable food, suitable medicine and a qualified attendant.

236
00:17:09,137 --> 00:17:16,140
[SPEAKER_02]: May he meet the leader of the village and may not encounter calamity and disaster right here.

237
00:17:17,035 --> 00:17:29,485
[SPEAKER_02]: And so, too, when a person bodily... a person's bodily and verbal behavior are impure, he does not gain from time to time an opening of mind, a placidity of mind on that occasion, one should arouse sheer compassion.

238
00:17:30,787 --> 00:17:35,490
[SPEAKER_00]: And, eh... By the way, whose translation is that?

239
00:17:36,011 --> 00:17:36,992
[SPEAKER_01]: Eh... Bhikkhu Bodhi's.

240
00:17:37,292 --> 00:17:37,552
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

241
00:17:37,812 --> 00:17:38,133
[SPEAKER_01]: Mm-hm.

242
00:17:40,394 --> 00:17:41,876
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, that's kind of self-explanatory.

243
00:17:42,436 --> 00:17:42,937
[SPEAKER_00]: Mm-hm.

244
00:17:43,037 --> 00:17:44,938
[SPEAKER_00]: A person might not see that it's for their welfare.

245
00:17:45,419 --> 00:17:45,679
[SPEAKER_00]: Mm-hm.

246
00:17:46,304 --> 00:17:52,426
[SPEAKER_00]: To be, you know, restrained and develop the mind and calmness and so on.

247
00:17:54,126 --> 00:17:57,787
[SPEAKER_00]: So you try to help them and so they can see that it's for their own good.

248
00:17:58,087 --> 00:18:00,048
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

249
00:18:00,068 --> 00:18:01,268
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's all you can do with.

250
00:18:01,468 --> 00:18:07,990
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why even the Buddha said, even in his, you know, he's the Buddha himself, the Supreme Buddha himself, enlightening in every way.

251
00:18:08,110 --> 00:18:11,031
[SPEAKER_00]: And he said, all I can do is point in the direction.

252
00:18:11,600 --> 00:18:11,920
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

253
00:18:13,481 --> 00:18:14,001
[SPEAKER_02]: I've just seen...

254
00:18:14,041 --> 00:18:21,826
[SPEAKER_02]: I've seen this particular part of the sutta quoted sometimes in regard to, like, the well-wishing, like... Well, that's the thing, you see, like... Yeah.

255
00:18:22,887 --> 00:18:23,327
[SPEAKER_00]: You would...

256
00:18:26,409 --> 00:18:35,534
[SPEAKER_00]: In order for you to do the well-wishing, you have to forget and ignore and be unaware and non-discerning of the fact that you are already compassionate.

257
00:18:38,536 --> 00:18:39,216
[SPEAKER_00]: Most people,

258
00:18:39,660 --> 00:18:44,122
[SPEAKER_00]: You already have a recognition of what compassion and helpfulness and friendliness is.

259
00:18:45,022 --> 00:18:58,027
[SPEAKER_00]: But practicing that, it's more like discerning the nature of those phenomena, not like emulating now this whole new artificial method of compassion on top of it, so you can somehow reconnect with it.

260
00:18:58,807 --> 00:19:00,228
[SPEAKER_00]: You're doing it instead of it.

261
00:19:01,088 --> 00:19:07,191
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, the more you commit to this ridiculous method of, how are you feeling now, I'm so concerned about you,

262
00:19:10,156 --> 00:19:20,161
[SPEAKER_00]: You will be covering up the natural compassion, which won't just say things that are compassionate, but will actually have a compassionate perspective, and say things that are helpful.

263
00:19:21,402 --> 00:19:24,903
[SPEAKER_00]: They might not necessarily make that person feel better.

264
00:19:25,684 --> 00:19:30,526
[SPEAKER_00]: That's, again, when the doctor tells you what the medicine is, it might not be a pleasant thing to hear.

265
00:19:31,126 --> 00:19:32,047
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, this is the treatment.

266
00:19:33,047 --> 00:19:34,808
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's factually for your own good.

267
00:19:35,248 --> 00:19:38,470
[SPEAKER_00]: But if a doctor just pats you on the back, hugs you, cries with you,

268
00:19:39,285 --> 00:19:42,107
[SPEAKER_00]: Full of loving-kindness doesn't want to upset you, you know.

269
00:19:43,008 --> 00:19:44,809
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not for your own... it's not for your good, isn't it?

270
00:19:45,970 --> 00:19:49,292
[SPEAKER_00]: You'll die ill, because treatment is too unpleasant to admit.

271
00:19:50,833 --> 00:20:03,202
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's... that would explain... like if people ever actually make the effort to study the suttas, they would see that the Buddha was sometimes extremely, extremely harsh in his verbal

272
00:20:04,563 --> 00:20:07,845
[SPEAKER_00]: Telling off to the monks they were not practicing or getting careless.

273
00:20:08,186 --> 00:20:11,508
[SPEAKER_00]: And not like idiot monks, like monks that didn't know what they were doing.

274
00:20:12,949 --> 00:20:15,811
[SPEAKER_00]: But, you know, like monks that were already established in dhamma.

275
00:20:16,471 --> 00:20:17,932
[SPEAKER_00]: Like Venerable Ananda and others.

276
00:20:19,353 --> 00:20:23,476
[SPEAKER_00]: And you say, oh, how can somebody full of love speak like this?

277
00:20:23,676 --> 00:20:32,581
[SPEAKER_00]: And you don't even, you know, if that upsets someone who reads it, don't even go and read Vibhanga stories, where the monks broke the rules, how Buddha then spoke to them.

278
00:20:34,511 --> 00:20:35,091
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I mean.

279
00:20:35,111 --> 00:20:39,733
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, it's nothing to do with that samāmi love and spreading my joy to you.

280
00:20:40,273 --> 00:20:50,297
[SPEAKER_00]: It's more like, out of my friendliness, I can help you, and that's why I'm not sacrificing my own perspective, which is... you are not aware of.

281
00:20:50,317 --> 00:20:55,919
[SPEAKER_00]: So, see, like, the other man knows that there is the end of his suffering if he continues walking to the next village.

282
00:20:56,580 --> 00:20:57,800
[SPEAKER_00]: He will get the proper care.

283
00:20:58,701 --> 00:21:00,561
[SPEAKER_00]: The man who is stuck in between, he doesn't know that.

284
00:21:00,661 --> 00:21:02,242
[SPEAKER_00]: And he doesn't know that this man knows that.

285
00:21:04,177 --> 00:21:11,099
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, it can maybe sound arrogant to him that this man is telling him where to go, but if he makes the effort, he'll confirm for himself.

286
00:21:11,659 --> 00:21:15,320
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's like this was pure compassion, pure non-arrogance.

287
00:21:16,341 --> 00:21:17,701
[SPEAKER_00]: Because he didn't have to say that to me.

288
00:21:17,941 --> 00:21:18,701
[SPEAKER_00]: It's nothing to him.

289
00:21:19,482 --> 00:21:28,084
[SPEAKER_00]: He doesn't know me, doesn't love me, you know, doesn't even, like, want to, like, spend time with me, so to speak.

290
00:21:28,184 --> 00:21:29,045
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, he still helped me.

291
00:21:29,925 --> 00:21:30,545
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I mean.

292
00:21:30,585 --> 00:21:33,386
[SPEAKER_00]: Compassion and all these things, even jhānas, by the way, are

293
00:21:34,097 --> 00:21:39,278
[SPEAKER_00]: ...developed by undoing everything that blocks them, in your experience.

294
00:21:40,338 --> 00:21:46,899
[SPEAKER_00]: And one of the main reason... one of the main thing that blocks them is the idea of a method and performance and set of prescriptions.

295
00:21:46,919 --> 00:21:48,860
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this is how I do, this is how I do, this is how I do it.

296
00:21:49,820 --> 00:21:59,121
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, even like in Brahmavihāras, you know, people try to emulate and do it mechanically, this spreading of matter, as you can spread your friendliness, here, here, here.

297
00:21:59,921 --> 00:22:03,042
[SPEAKER_00]: But, how would you then spread upeka?

298
00:22:04,083 --> 00:22:10,206
[SPEAKER_00]: How can you spread, how can you beam and spread rays of indifference?

299
00:22:11,746 --> 00:22:12,467
[SPEAKER_00]: How does that work?

300
00:22:13,507 --> 00:22:15,708
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're beaming indifference.

301
00:22:17,088 --> 00:22:18,249
[SPEAKER_00]: Here's my indifference to you.

302
00:22:19,009 --> 00:22:20,210
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the framework in your mind.

303
00:22:20,990 --> 00:22:27,793
[SPEAKER_00]: If your mind is developed to the point of imperturbable equanimity, friend, nothing matters anymore.

304
00:22:28,693 --> 00:22:31,835
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing can perturb that mind, because it's seen in the right order.

305
00:22:34,146 --> 00:22:44,873
[SPEAKER_02]: I've seen it too, I guess, people or teachers prescribing like this, like, you know, if you feel hate, you need to get rid of it.

306
00:22:44,934 --> 00:22:52,939
[SPEAKER_00]: Because people are so much dependent in their lay life, generally, even monks actually, on having something to do.

307
00:22:53,680 --> 00:22:56,141
[SPEAKER_00]: If I have nothing to do, well, I'll start going mad.

308
00:22:57,802 --> 00:23:02,806
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, even when they take on practice of dhamma, it just becomes a new thing, which is something to do.

309
00:23:03,804 --> 00:23:11,130
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is my duty, my this, my method, my meditation technique, and all that, and you become dependent on having to do it.

310
00:23:12,471 --> 00:23:16,874
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why if you come across instruction, meditation, well, you can't do it.

311
00:23:17,195 --> 00:23:18,195
[SPEAKER_00]: You can only understand it.

312
00:23:19,176 --> 00:23:20,497
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is not on the level of doing.

313
00:23:20,597 --> 00:23:23,880
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's just... For many people, that's hard to accept.

314
00:23:24,600 --> 00:23:28,744
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that means they need to undo their compulsive dependence upon having something to do.

315
00:23:29,524 --> 00:23:30,325
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's frightening.

316
00:23:32,222 --> 00:23:35,304
[SPEAKER_00]: It undermines you existentially, which means you will have to feel that.

317
00:23:36,224 --> 00:23:42,887
[SPEAKER_00]: But those who are not afraid to do it, then they get to see the direction where the mindfulness and understanding and discernment and everything else that Buddha talks about is.

318
00:23:48,943 --> 00:24:10,090
[SPEAKER_02]: So, in regard to having to experience being around a difficult person, and then hatred or resentment arising in regard to them, instead of standing there, you know, in your mind, wishing, radiating... You don't, you can't, and you shouldn't try to get rid of that resentment, in a sense of this should not be arising, because it's already there.

319
00:24:10,470 --> 00:24:12,191
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't want it, obviously, yet it's there.

320
00:24:12,911 --> 00:24:17,733
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you now trying to go against it, while it's there, is just confirming its presence even more.

321
00:24:18,654 --> 00:24:21,375
[SPEAKER_00]: Instead, you want to include it in the right context.

322
00:24:22,075 --> 00:24:25,235
[SPEAKER_00]: Context that you would have had to have understood beforehand and developed.

323
00:24:25,975 --> 00:24:31,536
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't include it magically in the right context through a set of prescribed motions and mechanical methods.

324
00:24:32,797 --> 00:24:34,537
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's not what the context is.

325
00:24:35,497 --> 00:24:38,578
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to understand the context that can include resentment.

326
00:24:39,878 --> 00:24:40,858
[SPEAKER_00]: Context of friendship.

327
00:24:41,478 --> 00:24:45,359
[SPEAKER_00]: You can be upset with your friend, because he's saying something or he's acting

328
00:24:46,532 --> 00:24:48,193
[SPEAKER_00]: Inappropriately, whatever, that's upset.

329
00:24:48,713 --> 00:24:50,174
[SPEAKER_00]: But you still don't question your friendship.

330
00:24:51,915 --> 00:24:54,796
[SPEAKER_00]: And to that extent, you're still not losing perspective on your behavior.

331
00:24:56,457 --> 00:25:01,459
[SPEAKER_00]: Means even if your friend is acting like an idiot, you're not going to get upset and dismiss him.

332
00:25:01,619 --> 00:25:05,041
[SPEAKER_00]: You're trying to help him because he's still primarily within the framework of friendship.

333
00:25:06,281 --> 00:25:09,303
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, if he really pushes it, then you might dismiss the whole framework.

334
00:25:09,983 --> 00:25:14,665
[SPEAKER_00]: But in this case, you want to not dismiss the framework in regard to people who you don't even know.

335
00:25:16,199 --> 00:25:18,381
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you can still maintain the framework of friendliness.

336
00:25:19,241 --> 00:25:23,945
[SPEAKER_00]: People, animals, you don't even need the engagement on the level of personalities.

337
00:25:25,726 --> 00:25:31,010
[SPEAKER_00]: So, anything opposite of me is within the framework of friendliness that I cultivate.

338
00:25:31,811 --> 00:25:36,154
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if I experience resentment, it's within the framework of the friendliness I cultivate.

339
00:25:36,174 --> 00:25:38,155
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, it's not really the resentment that you act out.

340
00:25:39,416 --> 00:25:40,917
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, it will cease as a resentment.

341
00:25:41,138 --> 00:25:45,441
[SPEAKER_00]: It's going to be just a response of... No, this is a genuinely foolish behavior that is disagreeable.

342
00:25:46,560 --> 00:25:50,202
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, resentment is just seen as a disagreeable counterpart of that behavior.

343
00:25:51,203 --> 00:25:54,905
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's not taken personally and it's certainly not rooted in your anger or your will anymore.

344
00:25:57,026 --> 00:26:05,671
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's what the Buddha would say, like, sometimes, if they, if they, you know, if you get, like, paired with a fool, it's factual.

345
00:26:05,751 --> 00:26:06,771
[SPEAKER_00]: His behavior is foolish.

346
00:26:06,791 --> 00:26:07,552
[SPEAKER_00]: He's a foolish man.

347
00:26:07,592 --> 00:26:07,992
[SPEAKER_00]: You leave.

348
00:26:08,052 --> 00:26:09,073
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't want to deal with him.

349
00:26:11,614 --> 00:26:12,975
[SPEAKER_00]: But people, like, think, oh, it's just this...

350
00:26:14,588 --> 00:26:18,790
[SPEAKER_00]: Non-discriminating, all-encompassing, universal love towards everything.

351
00:26:18,830 --> 00:26:19,710
[SPEAKER_00]: I just love everything.

352
00:26:20,470 --> 00:26:21,391
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm in love with everything.

353
00:26:22,931 --> 00:26:26,353
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, I'm oblivious to the nature of action of that person.

354
00:26:26,573 --> 00:26:28,853
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't see how they behave, because I just love everyone.

355
00:26:28,873 --> 00:26:29,294
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't care.

356
00:26:30,534 --> 00:26:31,154
[SPEAKER_00]: No, you do care.

357
00:26:31,174 --> 00:26:33,875
[SPEAKER_00]: You're just trying to override it and pretend it's not there.

358
00:26:36,456 --> 00:26:37,557
[SPEAKER_02]: Is there a way of...

359
00:26:39,473 --> 00:26:46,958
[SPEAKER_02]: If you see that you've lost the context and you say, oh, I should not say anything, I should not do anything, I should walk away.

360
00:26:47,459 --> 00:26:47,919
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, exactly.

361
00:26:47,939 --> 00:26:49,400
[SPEAKER_00]: That is the extent of your...

362
00:26:49,500 --> 00:26:56,245
[SPEAKER_00]: If you have lost the context and you're certain that you cannot act out of the right perspective, then, yeah, you restrain.

363
